    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d319">

                <pb facs="0440" n="338" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg338"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/4754: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Eberhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-05-04T19:00:00-05:45"><hi rendition="#italic">May 4, 1927—7
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 10:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>123. Following from Stimson:</p>
                <p>“Your 83 received since my telegram 122,<anchor n="64" xml:id="d319-f1"/> which
                    will make clear the impossibility of taking a position as you suggest.
                    Supervision of 1928 elections has been throughout my negotiations, as my
                    despatches must have clearly revealed, the chief constructive proposition about
                    which I have been able to rally the support of all elements and by reason of
                    which Moncada and Sacasa delegates, although they have not formally agreed to
                    settlement, are trying to persuade their followers to a peaceful submission and
                    general peace. To now repudiate that offer would in my opinion be unthinkable
                    and a moral if not a technical breach of faith on the part’ of the United
                    States. Stimson.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Eberhardt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
